// Fixture for `dependency_named_main_keeps_the_entry_point.saw` (SL-305).
//
// A dependency that declares the name `main`. Nothing here is unusual: `main`
// is an ordinary identifier to every module but the entry one, and a library
// that grew a `main` helper (a state-machine driver, a REPL loop) has no reason
// to know the entry module is about to lose its C entry point over it.
//
// Design 249 took its module tag from a CENSUS of which modules declare each
// free-function name, and counted the root module like any other — so this
// declaration gave the ENTRY module's `main` a `$M$root` tag, the program
// emitted no `main` symbol at all, and the link failed with
// `Undefined symbols: "_main", referenced from: <initial-undefines>`: a bare
// linker error naming no user construct. SL-274 replaced the census with a
// per-module tag plus ONE exemption — the ENTRY module's `main`, and only that
// one — so this `main` is an ordinary free function with an ordinary
// `main$m$sl305dep` symbol and internal linkage.
//
// It SUSPENDS, so its coroutine frame is keyed by that symbol too: the entry
// module's `main` and this one must name two different frames, which is the
// half a symbol-only rename would have got wrong.

import std.task.{yield_now}

func main(n: Int) -> Int {
    yield_now()
    n + 1
}

/// The only public surface: nothing outside this module can even name `main`
/// here, which is why the collision was invisible at the source level.
public func reach(n: Int) -> Int { main(n) }
